      "writtenOn": "2022-12-09T19:10:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, these are valid declarations in our implementation. I should clarify that the `atomic_uint` that I introduced here is _not_ the same as the atomic counter opaque type in GLSL, which is more restrictive in where you can instantiate one.\n\nThe closest GLSL analog I could find that matches the type of behavior I wanted to capture is the set of atomic operations that act on SSBO and shared (i.e. workgroup) memory that are available in GLSL 4.30 and GLSL ES 3.1, for example: https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL-Refpages/gl4/html/atomicAdd.xhtml. This is much closer to how atomic operations are defined in SPIR-V, Metal, and WGSL.\n\nHowever, like in SPIR-V, they can operate on any type that is declared as `uint` or `int` whereas I wanted SkSL atomics to operate on a stronger type as they do in Metal and WGSL. Yet GLSL also defines the atomic counter type which doesn\u0027t exactly match what I wanted but gets the opaque/strong type portion right.\n\nIt doesn\u0027t help with the confusion that GLSL calls the \"atomic counter\" type `atomic_uint` and I also called the SkSL type that way. Metal defines the corresponding uint threadgroup/device memory atomic type as `atomic\u003cuint\u003e` and aliases it to `atomic_uint` (WGSL calls its version `atomic\u003cu32\u003e`).\n\nIn the end if I were adding support for the SkSL `atomic_uint` to the GLSL backend, I would probably want to emit a `uint` and translate the atomic operations to the ones I linked to and not the `atomicCounter*` ones.",
      "parentUuid": "c601cf1e_d25d274b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 8,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 13,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "bc08bfb6fd63e9d687627421c50cf16d6cd9ae85",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
